      "text": "To find the greatest possible value of \\( x \\) that is a positive multiple of 3 and such that \\( x^3 < 1000 \\), we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Determine the largest integer \\( n \\) for which \\( n^3 < 1000 \\).\n2. Identify the largest multiple of 3 that is less than this integer.\n\nFirst, we need to find the largest integer \\( n \\) such that \\( n^3 < 1000 \\). We can do this by calculating the cube roots of numbers and finding the largest integer whose cube is less than 1000.\n\nBreaking it down:\n\\[ 1^3 = 1 \\]\n\\[ 2^3 = 8 \\]\n\\[ 3^3 = 27 \\]\n\\[ 4^3 = 64 \\]\n\\[ 5^3 = 125 \\]\n\\[ 6^3 = 216 \\]\n\\[ 7^3 = 343 \\]\n\\[ 8^3 = 512 \\]\n\\[ 9^3 = 729 \\]\n\\[ 10^3 = 1000 \\]\n\nFrom this calculation, we see that \\( 9^3 = 729 \\) and \\( 10^3 = 1000 \\). Therefore, the largest integer \\( n \\) for which \\( n^3 < 1000 \\) is 9. The largest multiple of 3 that is less than 9 is 6.\n\nThus, the greatest possible value of \\( x \\) that is a positive multiple of 3 and satisfies \\( x^3 < 1000 \\) is \\( \\boxed{6} \\).",
